00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:57.710
So databases right now are
a cornerstone for research.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:01.950
Curated databases are a
tool that most [AUDIO OUT]

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.302
right now, on a day [AUDIO OUT].

00:03:04.302 --> 00:03:07.140
 

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.890
And many users
spend several hours

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:12.080
per week on various databases.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.140
When I was at
DictyBase, we surveyed

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.760
our users and many
people estimated

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.340
that they were spending probably
one day a week, over all,

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:25.863
on various resources.

00:03:25.863 --> 00:03:30.030
 

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.920
However, database
creation and maintenance

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:37.050
is an obscure process
to most users.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.180
Sorry-- I just need
to get rid of this.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:44.600
 

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.510
Yes, so database
creation and maintenance

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:48.480
is an obscure process
to most users.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.850
And this is what I want to
talk about to you today.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:55.970
So I am sure everybody
here has interacted

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:58.820
with many of these different
types of resources,

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.360
but this is to
give you an idea--

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.170
or to remind you-- of the
scope of curated resources

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:06.700
in the life sciences.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:10.040
So there is databases that
talk about protein functions,

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.200
genomes, chemicals, clinical
trials, interactions

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:17.100
with ligands, with
drugs, with proteins.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.224
There's a large
number of databases.

00:04:19.224 --> 00:04:23.770
 

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:31.280
In fact, the main reference, in
terms of biological databases,

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.740
is the yearly nucleic
acids research

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:42.680
database, which, in 2016, listed
1,665 different databases.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.610
And there's the link here
for the list of databases

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:48.195
that are compiled by
Michael Galperin, who's

00:04:48.195 --> 00:04:50.570
the editor for the Nucleic
Acids Research Database Issue.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:54.640
 

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:58.010
I want to point out that this
is not an exhaustive list of all

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:00.680
the databases that exist.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:05.310
The NAR database issue
selects resources

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.140
that are deemed of
high quality, that

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:11.710
are not redundant with
other existing resources.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:13.720
So in other words, if
you want to propose

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:16.470
a repackaging of
other databases,

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.470
it's usually not
going to make it

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:21.530
in the collection of databases.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:26.320
And also, they select
resources that are maintained.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:29.440
 

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.690
So just to say, there
is a large number

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:34.960
of what we call niche
databases, usually created

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.470
for specific projects, but
that are not maintained once,

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.040
for example, the students
or post-doc moves

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.769
onto a different project.

00:05:42.769 --> 00:05:45.352
So these are excluded from the
Nucleic Acids Research Database

00:05:45.352 --> 00:05:45.852
Issue.

00:05:45.852 --> 00:05:48.870
 

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:51.530
And even within this
collection, there's

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:55.330
still a small number that
goes obsolete every year.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:58.380
And so just as a
point to mention,

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:00.810
this year there
were 23 resources

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:02.690
that were removed,
because they didn't

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:05.700
seem to be maintained anymore.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:10.770
So today, I'm going to talk
about what is biocuration?

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:13.390
Why do biocuration?

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:17.540
And how do we do biocuration?

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:20.560
So to give a definition
of biocuration,

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:24.710
we can say that it's the
analysis, interpretation,

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:28.570
and integration of biological
information into data

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:30.010
repositories.

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:33.440
So it's, in part, data
capture, but that's

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.950
really just one aspect.

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:38.810
To do biocuration, you need
to understand the data,

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.110
and you need to
analyze it, and you

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:44.820
need to distill what the
researchers are trying

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:48.970
to show in order to translate
this into a language

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:51.940
that a computer will
be able to understand.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:54.780
And-- this is a
little subtlety--

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:59.720
without falling into the trap
of over interpreting data

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:03.210
when, for example, interesting
new theories are discussed

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:07.470
in papers, but there's not
the exact supporting evidence

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:10.670
that one needs to
really say that this is

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:12.730
an observation that
has been shown,

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:16.030
whereas  this is a
conclusion that we might

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:19.110
want to test in the future.

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:22.740
So this data is integrated
into some structure form--

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:25.720
usually it's a database, but
it doesn't really have to be.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:26.990
This is very technical.

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:31.170
I'm not going to focus
on the technical aspects.

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:36.080
And so really, biocuration
encompasses a wide range

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:37.540
of activities.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:39.890
So this definition
may not include

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:43.160
all the different
activities, but to give you

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:46.150
an idea of some of
the typical tasks

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:48.290
that a biocurator
might do-- it's

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:52.310
to conceptualize [AUDIO OUT]
certain types of data,

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:57.400
to create databases,
to evaluate data,

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.500
insert it in the database,
and also, if possible,

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:04.770
link it so that it makes
sense biologically.

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:06.590
And obviously store it.

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:09.230
 

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:12.770
So why do biocuration?

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:15.660
Right now, as we
know, modern research

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:18.960
generates huge
quantities of data.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:23.580
And while each data set is
very rich-- or each data point,

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:25.920
even-- is very rich on its own.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:28.040
Where it becomes
powerful and interesting

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:31.150
is when we can [AUDIO OUT]
the different data

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:33.860
from different groups from
different types of experiments,

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:35.169
and so on.

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:40.530
And so one aim of biocuration
and curated databases

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:44.560
is to be able to connect
and query all the knowledge

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.420
that we have generated as
a scientific community.

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:51.660
I mean, I think this is
anyways a common goal

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:53.365
of the entire
scientific community.

00:08:53.365 --> 00:08:56.050
 

00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:58.520
So why biocuration?

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:02.690
Is also because papers,
in the traditional form,

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:06.420
are not easily amenable
to computational analysis.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:10.090
So the data that is there,
the knowledge that is there,

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:12.350
is very difficult to extract.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:16.750
So it used to be that when there
was a little less literature

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:20.270
accumulated, people
could be expert and have

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:22.080
read everything in their field.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.194
But more and more, this is
becoming almost impossible.

00:09:25.194 --> 00:09:28.160
 

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:32.900
Another thing is that biological
data is very heterogeneous.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:35.050
So there's many
different techniques

00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:37.290
that ask similar questions.

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:41.640
New techniques are
constantly being developed.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:46.530
So one of the challenges
is also [AUDIO OUT] systems

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:49.670
where all this data can be a
little bit more interoperable.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:53.330
 

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:58.800
And so this is not exactly
built into how people currently

00:09:58.800 --> 00:09:59.990
do experiments.

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:05.730
So right now, we're at
a stage where the data

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:09.040
and the knowledge generated
by experiments cannot be

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:12.760
immediately consumed for
other types of analyses.

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:15.780
So right now, the process
is to convert this

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:19.720
into a form that
can be computed.

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:24.330
So the idea with
biocuration is that if we

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:28.870
have data that is
correctly captured, then

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:32.880
we can facilitate retrieval,
extraction, indexation,

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.860
and make the data, as
a whole, more valuable.

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:38.430
 

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:43.790
So what we're hoping for, that
a curated knowledge database

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:47.180
will provide, is
easy access to data.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:49.450
Data that is-- so I'm
repeating this a little bit,

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:53.200
but this is really
very important

00:10:53.200 --> 00:11:02.080
in terms of why we're doing
[AUDIO OUT] status [AUDIO OUT]

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:09.730
Also [AUDIO OUT] and
ideally the [AUDIO OUT]

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:13.920
The other thing is it
enables [AUDIO OUT].

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.980
And just a point on the
current [AUDIO OUT], sometimes

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:19.620
if you [AUDIO OUT],
and you haven't

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:23.010
read other articles that have
been published afterwards,

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:26.650
you don't know if what was
demonstrated in that article

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:34.130
is still the best [AUDIO OUT]
you're looking at.

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:36.580
So ideally, we would
have a way-- you know,

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:41.180
databases aim to provide the
current state of knowledge.

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:46.730
So data has been shown to
be incorrect or inaccurate

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:50.940
should be removed, or corrected.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:54.600
OK, so how do we do biocuration?

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:56.482
There is really [AUDIO OUT].

00:11:56.482 --> 00:11:59.250
 

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:01.360
The first one is manual,
and the other one

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:05.120
is automated, or assisted
with text mining.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:08.490
I'm going to describe
both approaches.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:13.140
But first, I'm going to
focus on the manual approach.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:16.150
So some of the
[AUDIO OUT] for-- oh yeah,

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:20.740
so I just want to say
that for today's talk,

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:23.590
I give some examples
of literature curation.

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:27.410
Obviously [AUDIO OUT]
there's many types

00:12:27.410 --> 00:12:28.760
of data that are curated.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:32.480
There is many type of databases,
like I mentioned earlier.

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:34.900
So for the examples
today, I'm going

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:39.860
to focus on literature
curation, just for simplicity.

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:42.360
Unfortunately, there
is no time to go over

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:44.800
all the different types of
curation that can be done.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:46.996
But one that comes
to mind, really

00:12:46.996 --> 00:12:48.370
very important
thing that I won't

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:51.710
be talking about this-- genome
annotation, gene models,

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:54.850
sequence features, and so on.

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:58.762
So here is an overview
of what a curator does,

00:12:58.762 --> 00:13:02.290
as specific steps,
when annotating

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:04.300
a paper from the literature.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:14.830
So first, you need what I
call bio-entities [AUDIO OUT]

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:15.840
that are in the paper.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:18.150
Because usually what
people want to do

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:24.190
is to connect [AUDIO OUT]
modification or a phenotype.

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:28.360
So you need to know what gene
is being described in the paper.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:33.180
[AUDIO OUT] seem trivial
to people doing research

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:34.800
and writing papers,
but when you're

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:38.330
on the other end-- reading
papers and trying to extract

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:44.250
this information-- in fact, it's
an extremely challenging task.

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:47.160
Of course, there is
trivial [AUDIO OUT].

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:53.260
For example, when a gene name
is the synonym of another gene--

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:57.760
so sometimes you
need to [AUDIO OUT]

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:01.300
a paper is talking about.

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:07.650
Issues of [AUDIO OUT]
that can be [AUDIO OUT]

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:12.064
describe variance, or even post
translational modification.

00:14:12.064 --> 00:14:17.290
[AUDIO OUT] And you
know [AUDIO OUT]

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:19.910
on the current
reference sequence

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:23.520
is different from the one that
was presented in the paper--

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:25.810
it's also to be resolved.

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:32.370
And also, [AUDIO OUT] it can be
difficult to find the species

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:34.900
that is described in a paper.

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:37.960
There's several cases
that we have encountered

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:41.960
where a paper talks
about a human process,

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:46.420
but somehow when you
read in very detail,

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:50.700
the materials and methods-- or
sometimes, even it has happened

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:53.370
that we had to do a
blast, and then we

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:55.930
realized that, in fact, it
was a mouse protein being

00:14:55.930 --> 00:14:57.570
expressed in a human cell.

00:14:57.570 --> 00:14:59.800
OK, so we just always
want to make sure

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:03.820
that whatever information
is extracted from the paper

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:08.300
is linked to the correct
biological entity.

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:11.610
So this is the first
thing-- is to just extract

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:15.840
which gene the paper, or genes
the paper is talking about.

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:18.820
Also, obviously,
you want to extract

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:21.790
whatever real
information knowledge is

00:15:21.790 --> 00:15:23.680
published in the paper.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:26.840
So for example, they show
a molecular function,

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:30.960
or they show a pathway
in which the gene is

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:32.750
involved in-- a
[? phosphorylation ?]

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:35.740
site, a phenotype, and so on.

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:39.270
And synthesize the
information to represent,

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:42.300
like I said, the current
state of knowledge.

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:46.410
So it also happens
that [AUDIO OUT]

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:51.060
occurs at the level of
detail presentation.

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:55.640
Here are some examples of
bio-entity recognition issues.

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:58.740
So maybe some of you
are familiar with APC,

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:04.740
which represents either a
protein involved in a colon

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:08.960
cancer, or the
anaphase-promoting complex.

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:11.560
So people familiar with that
literature would be immediately

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:13.560
know that these are
different kinds of papers.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:19.630
But it is not trivial for
everybody to recognize.

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:25.030
There's also, here I put
some drosophila genes,

00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:28.430
but it's not that it's
the only case where

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:32.370
there's genes that
are named with words

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:34.510
that are real English words.

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:38.690
And so, obviously,
this can also cause

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:42.400
a false negative
problems-- you're

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:44.650
looking for the
literature for that gene,

00:16:44.650 --> 00:16:48.240
and you find a lot of papers
that talk about white.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:50.940
But it's very difficult to
find the papers that talk

00:16:50.940 --> 00:16:53.340
about the gene, white.

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:55.300
And I mentioned in
the previous slide,

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:59.960
the sequence size of
[? foreign ?] species issues.

00:16:59.960 --> 00:17:03.690
But this something
that curators spend

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:10.349
a long time trying to sort
out, when annotating a paper.

00:17:10.349 --> 00:17:16.680
So to discuss a little bit the
second step of the biocuration

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:22.630
task [AUDIO OUT] I'm going to
give you an example of work

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:25.359
that we've done in my group.

00:17:25.359 --> 00:17:28.840
So we've curated the
functional impact

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:31.150
of variations in
the human genome

00:17:31.150 --> 00:17:34.420
for a number of cancer variants.

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:35.920
And so we were
interested in really

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:41.160
capturing the molecular
mechanism of these variants.

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:47.720
So we can [AUDIO OUT]
cellular processes,

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:50.920
and molecular
[AUDIO OUT] binding,

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:54.170
changes in [AUDIO OUT] or in
sub-cellular localization.

00:17:54.170 --> 00:17:57.130
 

00:17:57.130 --> 00:18:02.260
And this is the overview
of the MLH1.

00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:06.200
I'm sure many of you have
seen these types of databases

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:10.516
where you can go and
browse some information.

00:18:10.516 --> 00:18:14.380
[AUDIO OUT] Show you the
phenotypes of the gene, MLH1,

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:17.920
involved in colo-rectal cancer.

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:21.470
And at the first level, we
show the different phenotypes

00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:23.550
that are tested for this gene.

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:26.920
So there is-- I cannot
get my mouse to work here,

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:31.410
but we have divided
them by groups.

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:36.430
So the molecular
function-- [AUDIO OUT]

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:38.000
[? ATPA's ?] activity.

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:40.910
There is also impact
on biological process--

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:44.340
so the [AUDIO OUT]
is not [AUDIO OUT].

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:47.250
It's localization,
which is nuclear,

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:51.150
and impacts on some important
binary interactions.

00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:56.450
And you can go in and
search for any gene that

00:18:56.450 --> 00:19:01.480
has a defect in a specific
phenotype you're interested in.

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:04.830
So for example, you can look at
the list of variants that have

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:07.130
defects and mismatch repair.

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:12.220
And so here, there's four
different variants listed.

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:15.090
So this is a
partial [AUDIO OUT],

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:17.347
100 different variants
for this gene,

00:19:17.347 --> 00:19:20.920
with problems and
mismatch repair.

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:26.820
And so we also show all
the evidence for the data

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:28.060
that we're capturing.

00:19:28.060 --> 00:19:30.390
So this is, I think,
very important in terms

00:19:30.390 --> 00:19:32.950
of trusting the data.

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:37.350
Anybody who downloads
this list can then

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:39.310
go back to the
original literature,

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:43.310
and decide if you agree
with the information which

00:19:43.310 --> 00:19:44.280
is presented there.

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:48.730
 

00:19:48.730 --> 00:19:52.800
So this was the end
result. So going back

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:55.630
on how we actually
extract the information--

00:19:55.630 --> 00:20:00.456
so this is one of the articles
that we used to annotate MLH1.

00:20:00.456 --> 00:20:02.780
 

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:07.330
And so one of the challenges--
[AUDIO OUT] is dispersed.

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:10.220
In this case, there's
information dispersed

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:12.130
within the same table,
but sometimes it's

00:20:12.130 --> 00:20:14.930
even more widespread
in the paper.

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:19.410
So we have the gene name,
MLH1, [INAUDIBLE] of the title

00:20:19.410 --> 00:20:21.820
of the table, a
list of mutations,

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:26.640
and of [AUDIO OUT] in the table.

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:29.700
We also have the phenotypes
in which we're interested in,

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:31.630
that I highlighted in yellow.

00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:36.400
So the MSI status-- it stands
for microsatellite instability

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:37.330
status.

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:41.240
So this is something
that [AUDIO OUT] tumors,

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:44.170
and it measures
essentially whether or not

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:49.720
the mismatch repair system
is functional or not.

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:56.570
And in this case it's given a
qualitative value-- high, low,

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:59.390
or not tested.

00:20:59.390 --> 00:21:01.950
And then the other column we
were interested in capturing,

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:05.840
like I mentioned, is
the protein stability.

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:11.100
And so in this case, it's
tested by immunocytochemistry.

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:13.230
So we know this, because
we know that in this case,

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:16.596
immunocytochemistry
measures the level

00:21:16.596 --> 00:21:17.720
of the protein in the cell.

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:20.100
But obviously, you can
do immunocytochemistry

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:26.150
to measure many other things,
just a localization, and so on.

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:30.850
Also, there is, in
this table, a number

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:35.880
of variants that are not
original from this paper--

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:38.340
[? data. ?] So for
some reason, they

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:42.420
thought these were interesting,
and the authors added them

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:44.030
in their analysis.

00:21:44.030 --> 00:21:48.630
But the data wasn't [AUDIO OUT].

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:56.870
And so in these
cases, we [AUDIO OUT]

00:21:56.870 --> 00:22:01.680
just cited, because
we [AUDIO OUT].

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:05.630
We only capture each
experiment only once.

00:22:05.630 --> 00:22:08.510
If it's cited in a
secondary publication,

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:10.670
we don't capture it.

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:12.010
 

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:16.450
Because otherwise, it would
give artificial weight

00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:22.100
to the assertion, because it's
been found X number of times.

00:22:22.100 --> 00:22:29.210
So I think it's very important
that [AUDIO OUT] any time

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:33.670
something has been shown,
that that data doesn't

00:22:33.670 --> 00:22:37.860
get duplicated in the
annotation process.

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:43.020
This is a similar data,
a different paper.

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:49.160
So in this case,
they also [AUDIO OUT]

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:51.810
histochemistry, as
you see on the left.

00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:55.617
And they tested in what
they call step 2-- MMR

00:22:55.617 --> 00:23:00.370
assay-- which is also
testing mismatch repair.

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:02.510
It's very similar
data, it's just

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:05.800
presented in a
very different way.

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:11.090
So the legend says
that the black cells

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:15.210
represent pathogenic or
deficient [AUDIO OUT].

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:18.520
White cells are non-pathogenic.

00:23:18.520 --> 00:23:21.960
The diagonal line means
that it hasn't been tested,

00:23:21.960 --> 00:23:28.240
and the gray cells mean that
there is conflicting data.

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:32.730
So this is something that,
manually, we can understand,

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:36.460
but that automatic
annotation systems,

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:41.300
I don't know of any system that
can extract the information

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:44.410
from such a table.

00:23:44.410 --> 00:23:48.260
Now this paper [AUDIO OUT]
you find quite frequently

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:57.850
in research papers,
is a small case f

00:23:57.850 --> 00:24:05.410
next to the variant
PK 618 alanine.

00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:11.040
So in the legend, it
says that these families

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:12.830
have additional mutations.

00:24:12.830 --> 00:24:16.410
So in this case, there's two
mutations in the same gene.

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:20.250
And so, of course,
[AUDIO OUT] footnote

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:25.410
may be very important in
terms of the explanation

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:26.720
of the phenotype.

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:28.890
So in this case, we're
looking at a double mutant,

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:33.200
but in the table it's
presented as a single mutant.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:37.650
So again, this is some
of the very detailed work

00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:40.390
that our team, and
many other teams,

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:42.450
does to make sure
that we extract

00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:43.890
all the information accurately.

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:46.970
 

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:54.290
Now, I mentioned that there's
a microsatellite instability,

00:24:54.290 --> 00:24:57.350
and the second paper
had MMR-- which

00:24:57.350 --> 00:25:01.350
is a mismatch repair assay.

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:03.380
There's different
types of experiments

00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:08.195
that test the same thing, in
this mismatch repair field.

00:25:08.195 --> 00:25:11.600
So in this case, I listed
four different experiments--

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:14.300
microsatellite instability,
in vitro mismatch repair

00:25:14.300 --> 00:25:18.071
assay, mutator phenotype
assay, [AUDIO OUT].

00:25:18.071 --> 00:25:20.760
 

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:24.110
These assays all reflect
defective mismatch repair

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:25.340
pathway.

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:27.360
So we believe that
it's very important

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:30.880
that these be captured
in the same manner,

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:33.725
[? or ?] that there is
four different [AUDIO OUT].

00:25:33.725 --> 00:25:36.640
 

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:41.410
To explain a little bit
more about the work on gene

00:25:41.410 --> 00:25:45.030
ontology, I think
many of you must

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:50.180
be familiar with gene ontology--
it's one of the most widely

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:54.010
used bioinformatics resource.

00:25:54.010 --> 00:25:56.980
It's a shared language
for a [? gene notation ?]

00:25:56.980 --> 00:26:03.100
of molecular
[AUDIO OUT] functions

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:05.950
and biological process
and cellular components

00:26:05.950 --> 00:26:08.530
across different organisms.

00:26:08.530 --> 00:26:11.210
And it covers three different
domains, like I just said--

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:13.900
functional, process, component.

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:16.920
And [AUDIO OUT]
developed this as exactly

00:26:16.920 --> 00:26:19.530
to be able to standardize the
description of gene function.

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:22.110
 

00:26:22.110 --> 00:26:26.080
Like I just said, biocurators
link gene, or gene products,

00:26:26.080 --> 00:26:31.900
or modified proteins and so on--
two times of the gene ontology,

00:26:31.900 --> 00:26:35.780
or various ontologies,
in order for the data

00:26:35.780 --> 00:26:38.150
to be organized in
a consistent way

00:26:38.150 --> 00:26:42.320
and for a user to be
able to consume it

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:45.460
in a way that is [AUDIO OUT].

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:48.910
So in terms of the MLH1
protein, for example,

00:26:48.910 --> 00:26:51.990
that I was mentioning,
its function

00:26:51.990 --> 00:26:55.210
is to bind another complex.

00:26:55.210 --> 00:26:57.200
It's involved in
mismatch repair,

00:26:57.200 --> 00:26:58.745
and it's localized
to the nucleus.

00:26:58.745 --> 00:27:05.610
OK, and it has a number of
other more subtle and gene

00:27:05.610 --> 00:27:11.100
ontology functions
annotations-- [AUDIO OUT]

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:14.310
gene ontology annotations
to function or process

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:14.920
a component.

00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:17.480
But these are the
most central ones.

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:20.140
 

00:27:20.140 --> 00:27:24.720
So I suppose that you might be
familiar with other biomedical

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:25.500
ontologies.

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:30.700
There is, right now, biomedical
ontologies in most areas.

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:35.140
So to describe anatomies,
chemicals, diseases,

00:27:35.140 --> 00:27:40.600
[AUDIO OUT] experiments-- so the
mass spectrometry community has

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:44.120
an ontology that describes the
different techniques that they

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:47.080
use.

00:27:47.080 --> 00:27:49.300
There's also--
yeah, [? indigo ?]

00:27:49.300 --> 00:27:53.440
can describe protein function,
and sub cellular component.

00:27:53.440 --> 00:27:57.780
Can describe phenotypes, can
describe proteins, and protein

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:00.830
families, sequence, and so on.

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:04.720
So this is really one way to
represent the data, that also

00:28:04.720 --> 00:28:08.770
has been proven [AUDIO OUT]
research community,

00:28:08.770 --> 00:28:13.570
if we just look at how much
the gene ontology is being used

00:28:13.570 --> 00:28:15.006
in bioinformatics applications.

00:28:15.006 --> 00:28:18.320
 

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:25.920
So one [AUDIO OUT] comment
that we often get about

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:31.743
by manual biocuration is
that it [AUDIO OUT] it's

00:28:31.743 --> 00:28:35.640
[AUDIO OUT] long and it
doesn't scale easily.

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:39.220
Although it's very
accurate, we need

00:28:39.220 --> 00:28:44.110
to develop ways to make
manual annotation [AUDIO OUT].

00:28:44.110 --> 00:28:48.510
So I'm going to give you one
example in that direction.

00:28:48.510 --> 00:28:50.560
In the gene ontology
consortium, we

00:28:50.560 --> 00:28:54.070
have developed a tool called
PAINT-- phylogenetic-based

00:28:54.070 --> 00:28:55.800
annotation inference.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:29:00.150
And what you do is we make
gene ontology annotations

00:29:00.150 --> 00:29:02.931
[AUDIO OUT] relations.

00:29:02.931 --> 00:29:04.890
So I'm just going to
spend a couple of slides

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:06.250
describing this.

00:29:06.250 --> 00:29:11.730
Here what we have on the
left is a gene family

00:29:11.730 --> 00:29:15.510
for MSH2, which is another
member of the mismatch repair

00:29:15.510 --> 00:29:16.510
complex.

00:29:16.510 --> 00:29:24.120
So there's proteins-- this is
conserved from human to E coli.

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:32.250
And so the sequences in green
are experiments [AUDIO OUT]

00:29:32.250 --> 00:29:34.150
by other groups.

00:29:34.150 --> 00:29:38.240
And [? again, ?]
do, using this is we

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:42.260
look at the relationships
of all these genes,

00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:46.310
and then we can decide where
a function, first of all.

00:29:46.310 --> 00:29:51.250
And then we, what we
call, propagate or infer

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:57.180
that function to a node--
a phylogenetic ancestor

00:29:57.180 --> 00:29:58.920
that we choose.

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:02.130
And [AUDIO OUT]
is the second step

00:30:02.130 --> 00:30:06.170
of the annotation, where
from this ancestor,

00:30:06.170 --> 00:30:10.430
the annotations are propagated
to all the ones and blue,

00:30:10.430 --> 00:30:12.760
the ones that didn't
have any annotations.

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:18.710
So the workflow is to look at
the experimental annotations

00:30:18.710 --> 00:30:20.870
shown here in blue.

00:30:20.870 --> 00:30:24.980
And so essentially
the process is

00:30:24.980 --> 00:30:27.730
we look for the most
recent common ancestor,

00:30:27.730 --> 00:30:32.790
and then we decide if we want
to annotate to that [AUDIO OUT],

00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:35.860
or if we think that
it should be annotated

00:30:35.860 --> 00:30:40.020
to a more recent ancestor,
or a more ancient ancestor.

00:30:40.020 --> 00:30:43.690
And so then from
[AUDIO OUT] these decisions

00:30:43.690 --> 00:30:48.580
include, for example, sequence
conservation, active site

00:30:48.580 --> 00:30:53.740
presence or absence, and
a number of other things.

00:30:53.740 --> 00:30:59.880
And so I don't know if you can
see this alignment, probably,

00:30:59.880 --> 00:31:03.750
but we can also annotate
loss of function.

00:31:03.750 --> 00:31:07.100
So it happens in big
multi-gene families

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:09.250
that certain functions are lost.

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:13.410
In this case, it's because
an active site is lost.

00:31:13.410 --> 00:31:19.060
So this cysteine
[AUDIO OUT] PGM5,

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:23.470
but in the PGM1 [INAUDIBLE],
this cysteine is mutated

00:31:23.470 --> 00:31:26.040
to [? NSR ?] gene.

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:29.600
And therefore their
function is low

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:33.910
after this duplicate event
indicated in the green circle.

00:31:33.910 --> 00:31:38.284
And so using this,
[AUDIO OUT] to say that there

00:31:38.284 --> 00:31:39.450
has been a loss of function.

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:42.010
 

00:31:42.010 --> 00:31:44.350
And so this is to
give you some idea

00:31:44.350 --> 00:31:47.860
of how much more we
can do, using this more

00:31:47.860 --> 00:31:51.580
efficient curation system.

00:31:51.580 --> 00:31:57.630
So as of last year, we had 2,500
different families curated--

00:31:57.630 --> 00:32:00.950
so the families are based
on patho [? DB. ?] Right now

00:32:00.950 --> 00:32:05.120
there's a [AUDIO OUT] families
in the whole [? patho DB ?]

00:32:05.120 --> 00:32:09.640
set, so we've done
about a quarter.

00:32:09.640 --> 00:32:14.260
And within those
2,500 families, there

00:32:14.260 --> 00:32:19.860
has been over 5,000 human
genes annotated, and 20,000

00:32:19.860 --> 00:32:22.810
new human gene annotations.

00:32:22.810 --> 00:32:26.120
And if you look at,
also, all the organisms,

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:30.940
we have nearly 1 million
annotations with this system.

00:32:30.940 --> 00:32:33.140
This scales quite
well in the sense

00:32:33.140 --> 00:32:38.980
that one curator can do
about 1,000 families a year.

00:32:38.980 --> 00:32:42.295
Which, if you take the average,
it corresponds more or less

00:32:42.295 --> 00:32:46.520
to 100,000 genes.

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:48.750
So the only drawback
is that, of course,

00:32:48.750 --> 00:32:50.950
we still need the primary
annotations in order

00:32:50.950 --> 00:32:52.310
to be able to do this.

00:32:52.310 --> 00:32:55.795
But the nice thing is,
it annotates multiple new

00:32:55.795 --> 00:32:57.900
[AUDIO OUT] no annotations.

00:32:57.900 --> 00:33:01.050
 

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:03.530
So now I'm going to
switch gears a little bit,

00:33:03.530 --> 00:33:05.440
to talk about
automated curation.

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:11.440
 

00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:16.010
So the manual curation is
very nice, but like I said,

00:33:16.010 --> 00:33:17.195
it's time consuming.

00:33:17.195 --> 00:33:19.710
 

00:33:19.710 --> 00:33:23.710
And there's a lot of hope
that some automated systems

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:28.580
could help us do curation
more effectively.

00:33:28.580 --> 00:33:31.950
So the task that
automated curation tools

00:33:31.950 --> 00:33:36.490
that I'm aware of do, include
document retrieval, document

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:40.730
prioritization, what
they call-- so let's

00:33:40.730 --> 00:33:42.760
see if you're interested
in a specific gene,

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:47.170
a specific disease,
it can retrieve

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:53.330
documents that are relevant
to that gene disease process.

00:33:53.330 --> 00:33:56.946
These tools [AUDIO OUT]
these document,

00:33:56.946 --> 00:34:00.030
[AUDIO OUT] which ones
are more likely to be

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:03.040
more interesting to you.

00:34:03.040 --> 00:34:05.530
Gene normalization
task is to try

00:34:05.530 --> 00:34:09.850
to identify which gene the
paper is talking about.

00:34:09.850 --> 00:34:14.010
So this task, as far as I know--
maybe there's been more recent

00:34:14.010 --> 00:34:17.560
developments where
this is [AUDIO OUT]--

00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:20.560
as I explained
earlier in my talk,

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:22.900
even manually we
have a lot of issues.

00:34:22.900 --> 00:34:27.310
So the automatic systems who are
not usually performing really

00:34:27.310 --> 00:34:30.179
well in this task.

00:34:30.179 --> 00:34:34.020
And finally, another task
is concept identification.

00:34:34.020 --> 00:34:39.360
So which gene is mentioned
in the paper, or [AUDIO OUT]

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:41.300
mentioned in the paper?

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:41.940
And so on.

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:45.730
So these are all tasks that
we can ask automated systems

00:34:45.730 --> 00:34:48.420
to help us with.

00:34:48.420 --> 00:34:52.610
One very popular tool in
the biocuration community,

00:34:52.610 --> 00:34:56.580
for automatic annotation,
is called Textpresso.

00:34:56.580 --> 00:35:01.490
It's developed by the
group [AUDIO OUT},

00:35:01.490 --> 00:35:06.070
who also is [? PI ?] of
the one base database.

00:35:06.070 --> 00:35:09.060
And so it was first
implemented in one base,

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:14.190
but it's now in more and
more frequently being

00:35:14.190 --> 00:35:17.770
implemented in other
[AUDIO OUT] in DictyBase,

00:35:17.770 --> 00:35:20.060
and the Arabidopsis
thaliana resource.

00:35:20.060 --> 00:35:23.860
 

00:35:23.860 --> 00:35:27.990
So with Texpresso
can do is, if it's

00:35:27.990 --> 00:35:32.450
given on the left,
a number of PDFs

00:35:32.450 --> 00:35:36.060
of relevant literature, gene
product names and synonyms

00:35:36.060 --> 00:35:39.140
that it can use a search base.

00:35:39.140 --> 00:35:42.370
And while for [INAUDIBLE] and
plant [? cellular ?] component

00:35:42.370 --> 00:35:45.530
[? times. ?] So essentially
you have your literature,

00:35:45.530 --> 00:35:50.570
and your list of genes that
you're interested in-- what

00:35:50.570 --> 00:35:54.540
Textpresso does is it
converts the PDF to text,

00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:57.910
it tries to identify the
gene, gene product names.

00:35:57.910 --> 00:36:02.020
It marks up the text of
the paper, it indexes it.

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:04.735
And then it presents it to the
curator in a way [INAUDIBLE].

00:36:04.735 --> 00:36:06.110
I'm just going to
show you-- this

00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:10.780
is from the 2012 papers
that describe implementation

00:36:10.780 --> 00:36:16.560
of Texpresso in DictyBase
and in [AUDIO OUT].

00:36:16.560 --> 00:36:21.240
And so this is the
curation interface.

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:26.460
So at the top is title and
abstract of the of the paper.

00:36:26.460 --> 00:36:30.890
And then at the bottom, you
see the gene protein name

00:36:30.890 --> 00:36:32.400
that the system extracted.

00:36:32.400 --> 00:36:39.440
The gene ontology cellular
component term, nuclear,

00:36:39.440 --> 00:36:41.540
that it found in
this [AUDIO OUT].

00:36:41.540 --> 00:36:45.050
Then you can also select more
specific or less specific

00:36:45.050 --> 00:36:47.190
term [AUDIO OUT].

00:36:47.190 --> 00:36:50.950
And then the curator just sees
this pre-processed annotation,

00:36:50.950 --> 00:36:56.670
and can hit Make the Annotation,
or Don't Make the Annotation,

00:36:56.670 --> 00:36:58.940
and this goes directly
in the database.

00:36:58.940 --> 00:37:01.620
 

00:37:01.620 --> 00:37:09.390
Now, in automatic annotation,
the gold standard measures

00:37:09.390 --> 00:37:12.760
are precisions and
recall

00:37:12.760 --> 00:37:15.780
are the fraction of the
documents retrieved that are

00:37:15.780 --> 00:37:18.330
relevant to your query.

00:37:18.330 --> 00:37:24.620
So how much of
your [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:24.620 --> 00:37:27.520
did you really find?

00:37:27.520 --> 00:37:29.730
And the precision
is the fraction

00:37:29.730 --> 00:37:31.370
of the documents
that are retrieved,

00:37:31.370 --> 00:37:33.020
and that are relevant.

00:37:33.020 --> 00:37:37.190
Because the system obviously
can also give you some false

00:37:37.190 --> 00:37:37.970
positives.

00:37:37.970 --> 00:37:41.030
So their recall is-- it
measures, more or less,

00:37:41.030 --> 00:37:47.720
the false negatives [AUDIO OUT]
or the false positives.

00:37:47.720 --> 00:37:51.020
And so this is, again,
from the paper-- so

00:37:51.020 --> 00:37:55.460
for DictyBase and TAIR, the
recall was maximum [AUDIO OUT].

00:37:55.460 --> 00:37:58.390
So there's still 50%
of the literature

00:37:58.390 --> 00:38:02.940
that the curators needed to go
back to and annotate manually.

00:38:02.940 --> 00:38:09.480
And the precision was
between 60% and 75%.

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:13.430
So sometimes there were a
number of false positives, too.

00:38:13.430 --> 00:38:18.420
But despite that, here
I call this [AUDIO OUT]

00:38:18.420 --> 00:38:20.120
cost reduction.

00:38:20.120 --> 00:38:22.870
That's not how they phrased it
in the paper, but in the paper

00:38:22.870 --> 00:38:28.160
they say, a 2.5 fold increase
in curation efficiency.

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:31.650
So it's still pretty good--
you can annotate more than two

00:38:31.650 --> 00:38:36.410
times the number of papers that
you would without the system.

00:38:36.410 --> 00:38:40.120
The cons here, I would say
that usually these things don't

00:38:40.120 --> 00:38:46.210
take into [AUDIO OUT]
development [AUDIO OUT]

00:38:46.210 --> 00:38:49.930
of the infrastructure
that you need [AUDIO OUT].

00:38:49.930 --> 00:38:54.130
And also, there's
[AUDIO OUT] large projects

00:38:54.130 --> 00:39:00.760
in this area that [AUDIO OUT]
to [AUDIO OUT] a real life

00:39:00.760 --> 00:39:01.780
setting.

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:06.520
So right now, it's
still pretty embryonic,

00:39:06.520 --> 00:39:09.190
and there's lots of
research going on.

00:39:09.190 --> 00:39:14.020
But in [AUDIO OUT] able
to use automated methods,

00:39:14.020 --> 00:39:19.560
for biocuration, there is a lot
of tasks that cannot be done.

00:39:19.560 --> 00:39:23.200
 

00:39:23.200 --> 00:39:26.220
So we talked about
what, why, how--

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:31.360
and now I would like to talk
about who does biocuration.

00:39:31.360 --> 00:39:38.210
So as Jack mentioned, before
[AUDIO OUT] biocuration

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:41.760
is done by a professional
biocurator, such as me.

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:45.880
So we surveyed the
biocurators back in 2012,

00:39:45.880 --> 00:39:49.720
and there's a paper that
described who are these people,

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:52.340
and where they come from, and
why they became interested

00:39:52.340 --> 00:39:53.370
in this area.

00:39:53.370 --> 00:39:56.870
But just to say that,
most people have

00:39:56.870 --> 00:39:59.570
a lot of experience--
most are PhDs,

00:39:59.570 --> 00:40:04.026
most have a lot of experience
as bench scientists, so most

00:40:04.026 --> 00:40:07.260
[AUDIO OUT] and also post-docs.

00:40:07.260 --> 00:40:10.320
Some people have done
some programming,

00:40:10.320 --> 00:40:15.100
and usually people stay in
biocuration for a long time.

00:40:15.100 --> 00:40:18.340
So people are
experienced researchers,

00:40:18.340 --> 00:40:20.100
and experienced curators.

00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:24.410
 

00:40:24.410 --> 00:40:27.430
Also it was mentioned
that the biocurators have

00:40:27.430 --> 00:40:29.870
a professional society,
called the International

00:40:29.870 --> 00:40:35.740
Society for Biocuration-- and
[AUDIO OUT] different members

00:40:35.740 --> 00:40:39.490
that represents about 150
different institutions.

00:40:39.490 --> 00:40:42.150
There is annual
international meetings

00:40:42.150 --> 00:40:46.810
that are very interesting
in terms of giving people

00:40:46.810 --> 00:40:51.390
new ideas, and new ways to do
things, and to collaborate.

00:40:51.390 --> 00:40:54.870
So I think these are really,
really good for the community.

00:40:54.870 --> 00:40:58.970
And it's amazing that
next year is already

00:40:58.970 --> 00:41:02.690
going to be the 10th annual
meeting of the International

00:41:02.690 --> 00:41:03.690
Society for Biocuration.

00:41:03.690 --> 00:41:08.160
 

00:41:08.160 --> 00:41:11.540
So this is a bit
restating what I

00:41:11.540 --> 00:41:14.390
said at the beginning in
terms of the importance

00:41:14.390 --> 00:41:18.700
of curated resources, but
from the point of view

00:41:18.700 --> 00:41:21.390
of the biocurators, themselves.

00:41:21.390 --> 00:41:23.340
So what you would
like to have is

00:41:23.340 --> 00:41:25.290
to have compatible
infrastructure

00:41:25.290 --> 00:41:29.120
for data deposition,
exchange, and analysis.

00:41:29.120 --> 00:41:32.710
So it's important that we have
common controlled vocabularies,

00:41:32.710 --> 00:41:39.065
compatible tools, compatible
identifiers, data formats,

00:41:39.065 --> 00:41:42.170
[AUDIO OUT] systems, and so on.

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:45.140
At the same time, it's
[AUDIO OUT] balance, stability,

00:41:45.140 --> 00:41:46.890
and innovation.

00:41:46.890 --> 00:41:52.160
We don't want to be in a
situation where [AUDIO OUT] are

00:41:52.160 --> 00:41:55.110
so rigid that people cannot
start doing new types

00:41:55.110 --> 00:41:56.000
of experiments.

00:41:56.000 --> 00:41:58.090
So this is also
very challenging,

00:41:58.090 --> 00:42:01.310
to be that compatible

00:42:01.310 --> 00:42:04.080
and move forward
at the same time.

00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:08.030
We would like for the
resources that we're

00:42:08.030 --> 00:42:10.150
developing to be sustainable.

00:42:10.150 --> 00:42:16.220
And [AUDIO OUT] to get
researchers involved.

00:42:16.220 --> 00:42:19.900
So that's one aspect
of the one group

00:42:19.900 --> 00:42:22.820
of people who do biocuration.

00:42:22.820 --> 00:42:26.770
There's other
[AUDIO OUT] approach

00:42:26.770 --> 00:42:28.126
has been used [AUDIO OUT].

00:42:28.126 --> 00:42:31.970
 

00:42:31.970 --> 00:42:36.820
So crowdsourcing can be divided
into three different aspects.

00:42:36.820 --> 00:42:43.445
One is [AUDIO OUT] scientists to
contribute to your [AUDIO OUT].

00:42:43.445 --> 00:42:49.110
There's a wikigenes-- I know
back seven, 10 years ago,

00:42:49.110 --> 00:42:51.470
most of the
[? multi-organisms ?] databases

00:42:51.470 --> 00:42:57.560
had submitted their wiki to
try to get researchers to add

00:42:57.560 --> 00:42:59.700
their own data if they wanted.

00:42:59.700 --> 00:43:03.490
Rfam is one of the best
example of a very [AUDIO OUT].

00:43:03.490 --> 00:43:07.150
 

00:43:07.150 --> 00:43:09.980
The second approach
in crowdsourcing

00:43:09.980 --> 00:43:15.980
is [AUDIO OUT] that people
might know as [AUDIO OUT] Amazon

00:43:15.980 --> 00:43:17.170
perks.

00:43:17.170 --> 00:43:21.000
So you can go to Amazon
and propose-- I mean,

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:25.350
that's one [AUDIO OUT], but
you propose a very small task,

00:43:25.350 --> 00:43:30.360
and people are paid a
small amount to do one unit

00:43:30.360 --> 00:43:36.380
[INAUDIBLE] to identify
variance in an abstract.

00:43:36.380 --> 00:43:40.390
So just to tell you, is this
string of text a variant,

00:43:40.390 --> 00:43:42.431
yes or no?

00:43:42.431 --> 00:43:46.870
[AUDIO OUT] paper that Jeanette
Harshman published about this.

00:43:46.870 --> 00:43:51.260
It was quite successful
for that small task.

00:43:51.260 --> 00:43:54.260
But then, of course, if you want
to do more complicated tasks,

00:43:54.260 --> 00:43:57.030
then you need to start
making bigger payments.

00:43:57.030 --> 00:44:00.060
And you also
[AUDIO OUT] where you

00:44:00.060 --> 00:44:03.100
control for the contributors,
their expertise,

00:44:03.100 --> 00:44:06.020
how many false positives
they give you, and so on.

00:44:06.020 --> 00:44:09.220
Like, what's the
quality of their work.

00:44:09.220 --> 00:44:12.620
There is also an
interesting idea

00:44:12.620 --> 00:44:17.700
in developing games that
could help somehow integrate

00:44:17.700 --> 00:44:20.550
data in a more structured way.

00:44:20.550 --> 00:44:25.000
So there's-- well, Foldit is not
exactly going into databases,

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:28.850
but for protein predictions,
was the most successful game

00:44:28.850 --> 00:44:34.030
in biology, where people--
biologists and other people--

00:44:34.030 --> 00:44:41.680
could try to find the best
structure for a protein whose

00:44:41.680 --> 00:44:43.820
structure was not known.

00:44:43.820 --> 00:44:46.840
There's also Cancer
Research UK, who

00:44:46.840 --> 00:44:51.410
developed a few games and--
well, other groups, [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:51.410 --> 00:44:53.170
among other people--
I don't know why

00:44:53.170 --> 00:44:55.660
I forgot to put his name there.

00:44:55.660 --> 00:45:00.010
So there's a few
ideas of getting

00:45:00.010 --> 00:45:03.020
people involved in a fun way.

00:45:03.020 --> 00:45:05.880
But it hasn't been
picked up-- it's

00:45:05.880 --> 00:45:09.660
not like everybody's playing
these bioinformatics game

00:45:09.660 --> 00:45:11.680
while they're waiting for
the bus, or something.

00:45:11.680 --> 00:45:15.170
At least not as far as I know.

00:45:15.170 --> 00:45:18.830
So the pros of
crowdsourcing are a lot

00:45:18.830 --> 00:45:21.930
of potential contributors.

00:45:21.930 --> 00:45:25.220
There is a significant
cost to developing

00:45:25.220 --> 00:45:27.890
all this infrastructure--
many of them,

00:45:27.890 --> 00:45:30.330
if they're not used
very much, well, it

00:45:30.330 --> 00:45:35.870
becomes money that was
invested for not much return.

00:45:35.870 --> 00:45:41.180
And mostly, at least the
reports I have looked at,

00:45:41.180 --> 00:45:45.140
describe pretty low
rates of participation.

00:45:45.140 --> 00:45:47.860
So that's too bad,
really, because it

00:45:47.860 --> 00:45:50.808
would be a really great.

00:45:50.808 --> 00:45:56.360
[? A ?] group which is very
important for biocuration

00:45:56.360 --> 00:45:59.360
as a whole, is researcher.

00:45:59.360 --> 00:46:03.500
Many research projects
involve some curation.

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:08.240
And that data has not always
contributed back to databases--

00:46:08.240 --> 00:46:11.420
or often it's not.

00:46:11.420 --> 00:46:15.310
There are some journals
that encourage researchers

00:46:15.310 --> 00:46:19.220
to provide data in formats
that can be directly inserted

00:46:19.220 --> 00:46:21.750
in databases.

00:46:21.750 --> 00:46:25.190
So for example, gen
ontology annotations,

00:46:25.190 --> 00:46:27.380
a few journals
request that.

00:46:27.380 --> 00:46:32.150
But, clearly, the incentives
are not yet there for people

00:46:32.150 --> 00:46:35.970
to really provide a lot of data.

00:46:35.970 --> 00:46:38.780
Researchers have a lot
of other [AUDIO OUT].

00:46:38.780 --> 00:46:43.890
There is not much
recognition for having

00:46:43.890 --> 00:46:46.130
provided your data or not.

00:46:46.130 --> 00:46:50.120
So we really need
to work on providing

00:46:50.120 --> 00:46:52.050
better incentives
for researchers

00:46:52.050 --> 00:46:54.660
to curate their own data.

00:46:54.660 --> 00:46:58.960
And one obvious way
that I can think

00:46:58.960 --> 00:47:03.460
is-- and that's
discussed a lot lately,

00:47:03.460 --> 00:47:08.480
it's not my idea--
is to give some

00:47:08.480 --> 00:47:15.110
either via your [? arch ID ?]
or some other mechanism,

00:47:15.110 --> 00:47:19.431
that curated data would
receive some citable unit.

00:47:19.431 --> 00:47:22.380
 

00:47:22.380 --> 00:47:25.820
So the last little
bit of the talk,

00:47:25.820 --> 00:47:28.130
I'm going to discuss
versioning issues.

00:47:28.130 --> 00:47:31.070
 

00:47:31.070 --> 00:47:34.690
So because all these
curated databases

00:47:34.690 --> 00:47:38.120
are available out there, there's
more and more experiments

00:47:38.120 --> 00:47:41.320
that rely on these resources
for data analysis--

00:47:41.320 --> 00:47:45.090
especially in the
high throughput area.

00:47:45.090 --> 00:47:50.880
However, most papers that I read
that do bioinformatics analysis

00:47:50.880 --> 00:47:55.240
do not provide enough data to
reproduce their experiment,

00:47:55.240 --> 00:47:58.370
their data analysts experiment.

00:47:58.370 --> 00:47:59.757
So that's one issue.

00:47:59.757 --> 00:48:03.170
 

00:48:03.170 --> 00:48:07.680
This is [AUDIO OUT]
likely different issue

00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:13.320
is, I mentioned that
biocurated resources want

00:48:13.320 --> 00:48:17.610
to be up to date, with respect
to their current knowledge.

00:48:17.610 --> 00:48:20.350
So this is an example
of a case where

00:48:20.350 --> 00:48:22.990
we have a protein that
is a [? diomer. ?]

00:48:22.990 --> 00:48:26.860
And when the A subunit
is down to the B subunit,

00:48:26.860 --> 00:48:32.045
the A subunit inhibit the
activity of the B [AUDIO OUT].

00:48:32.045 --> 00:48:34.980
Now, there is a
protease that comes in,

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:38.150
and that degrades the A
subunit, and the B subunit

00:48:38.150 --> 00:48:39.150
becomes active.

00:48:39.150 --> 00:48:42.480
So this is not a very
complicated mechanism

00:48:42.480 --> 00:48:44.270
of regulation.

00:48:44.270 --> 00:48:47.220
So the A subunit is
involved in regulation.

00:48:47.220 --> 00:48:50.980
The B subunit-- has
it's a nuclease,

00:48:50.980 --> 00:48:53.710
it has the
deoxyribonuclease activity.

00:48:53.710 --> 00:48:55.350
Now, the reason
why I'm saying this

00:48:55.350 --> 00:49:00.220
is that, we didn't know
that it was a complex.

00:49:00.220 --> 00:49:05.410
We had only identified
the A subunit.

00:49:05.410 --> 00:49:09.850
And they thought that the
A subunit had the activity.

00:49:09.850 --> 00:49:12.800
So when you read the
[INAUDIBLE] literature,

00:49:12.800 --> 00:49:15.180
then you realize
that the resolution

00:49:15.180 --> 00:49:18.250
that the first assays
were not good enough,

00:49:18.250 --> 00:49:23.550
and their results were--
I guess you can say wrong,

00:49:23.550 --> 00:49:24.675
but it was just misleading.

00:49:24.675 --> 00:49:27.230
That the way they
did the experiment,

00:49:27.230 --> 00:49:30.960
they didn't have the resolution
to determine whether that there

00:49:30.960 --> 00:49:32.860
were two subunits there.

00:49:32.860 --> 00:49:38.791
So the idea is that,
to remove this--

00:49:38.791 --> 00:49:40.790
to remove the incorrect
annotation, to make sure

00:49:40.790 --> 00:49:43.730
that the knowledge
is kept up to date.

00:49:43.730 --> 00:49:46.140
And where I want
to go with this is

00:49:46.140 --> 00:49:50.200
this is just an example--
like a very specific example--

00:49:50.200 --> 00:49:53.840
but I want to show you
that annotations evolved.

00:49:53.840 --> 00:49:57.660
Annotation evolved
quite a bit, in fact.

00:49:57.660 --> 00:49:59.920
So here there is a tool
developed by [? Paltov ?]

00:49:59.920 --> 00:50:03.690
[? Lidis, ?] at the University
of British Columbia,

00:50:03.690 --> 00:50:08.790
where you can query either a
gene, or a gene ontology term,

00:50:08.790 --> 00:50:12.040
and see how it
changes over time.

00:50:12.040 --> 00:50:16.980
And in this case, I just picked
a protein kinase activity,

00:50:16.980 --> 00:50:21.170
and we see for different
species, the number

00:50:21.170 --> 00:50:29.600
of annotations over time,
since 2002-- [AUDIO OUT]

00:50:29.600 --> 00:50:32.720
of annotations anyways, only
human-annotated protein kinase

00:50:32.720 --> 00:50:33.590
activity.

00:50:33.590 --> 00:50:38.420
But it's not, as one might
expect, a linear increase.

00:50:38.420 --> 00:50:42.980
There's also time where there
is a big drop, or big increase.

00:50:42.980 --> 00:50:46.990
And so that means
that depending on when

00:50:46.990 --> 00:50:50.140
you do your bioinformatics
experiment using this data,

00:50:50.140 --> 00:50:52.115
your results can change
quite dramatically.

00:50:52.115 --> 00:50:55.120
 

00:50:55.120 --> 00:50:57.710
And to illustrate
this, there was a paper

00:50:57.710 --> 00:51:01.910
published just a
few [AUDIO OUT],

00:51:01.910 --> 00:51:07.500
that shows the impact of
outdated gene annotation

00:51:07.500 --> 00:51:09.770
on pathway enrichment analysis.

00:51:09.770 --> 00:51:14.750
So for high
throughput experiment,

00:51:14.750 --> 00:51:17.910
pathway enrichment analysis
is really something

00:51:17.910 --> 00:51:22.430
that people almost always
present in the paper.

00:51:22.430 --> 00:51:24.620
And what they've
shown here is they

00:51:24.620 --> 00:51:29.420
looked at a number of
different articles--

00:51:29.420 --> 00:51:35.170
so they took annotations
from 2010 and [AUDIO OUT] 16,

00:51:35.170 --> 00:51:39.270
and checked from these
different data sets, which

00:51:39.270 --> 00:51:41.410
terms were most enriched.

00:51:41.410 --> 00:51:47.640
And so the purple dots are
2016, and blue dots are 2000--

00:51:47.640 --> 00:51:52.120
I'm sorry, yellow are
2010, and blue dots

00:51:52.120 --> 00:51:55.410
are the ones that are
found in [? both. ?]

00:51:55.410 --> 00:52:00.030
So you see that there is
a number of [AUDIO OUT]

00:52:00.030 --> 00:52:04.230
between 2010 and now.

00:52:04.230 --> 00:52:06.740
And you also see,
with the purple dots,

00:52:06.740 --> 00:52:08.740
that there are many
times that are now

00:52:08.740 --> 00:52:11.050
enriched that didn't
used to be enriched,

00:52:11.050 --> 00:52:14.270
if you use the
2010 [? go ?] data.

00:52:14.270 --> 00:52:23.410
So it's very important
to know which version

00:52:23.410 --> 00:52:28.850
of the two [INAUDIBLE] of
the data that you're using.

00:52:28.850 --> 00:52:31.710
And so here is just
a few [AUDIO OUT]

00:52:31.710 --> 00:52:34.180
best practices for
bioinformatics experiments

00:52:34.180 --> 00:52:36.740
should be.

00:52:36.740 --> 00:52:40.850
People who use curated
resources and associated tools,

00:52:40.850 --> 00:52:44.660
should include publication
dates and version number

00:52:44.660 --> 00:52:47.510
of their tools, so the
date of the data set

00:52:47.510 --> 00:52:49.620
and version number
of their tools.

00:52:49.620 --> 00:52:53.310
And also, I find
sometimes in papers,

00:52:53.310 --> 00:52:55.330
people make
additional annotations

00:52:55.330 --> 00:52:58.110
because they found
it was incomplete.

00:52:58.110 --> 00:53:01.170
And so this information,
these additional annotations,

00:53:01.170 --> 00:53:04.320
are not always publicly
available.

00:53:04.320 --> 00:53:07.360
I would just like to
conclude by saying

00:53:07.360 --> 00:53:16.250
that biocuration should be an
integral part of the data life

00:53:16.250 --> 00:53:17.170
cycle.

00:53:17.170 --> 00:53:22.900
And so right now, it's often
that the curation occurs

00:53:22.900 --> 00:53:25.500
post publication--
but I'm really

00:53:25.500 --> 00:53:27.920
hoping that we
can get to a point

00:53:27.920 --> 00:53:32.570
where the biocuration will also
be part of the data management

00:53:32.570 --> 00:53:36.910
plan, part of the collection
plan, and part of the analysis.

00:53:36.910 --> 00:53:41.376
And that it won't just be
curated after the fact.

00:53:41.376 --> 00:53:42.550
So I'm going to stop here.

00:53:42.550 --> 00:53:45.510
I took a quite a bit of time.

00:53:45.510 --> 00:53:48.470
And I would like to
thank you for being here,

00:53:48.470 --> 00:53:53.650
and acknowledge the main
projects I'm involved with.

00:53:53.650 --> 00:53:57.560
Jack Van Horn: Thank you very much,
Pascale, that was fantastic.

00:53:57.560 --> 00:53:59.600
Rich talk.

00:53:59.600 --> 00:54:01.860
We've had a number of questions.

00:54:01.860 --> 00:54:04.220
We have a couple-- look
at a couple of the themes

00:54:04.220 --> 00:54:07.480
of the questions, they seem to
revolve around quality control,

00:54:07.480 --> 00:54:09.790
and how automated
quality control

00:54:09.790 --> 00:54:12.730
and verification [AUDIO OUT].

00:54:12.730 --> 00:54:16.630
They're also, when you're
doing a manual curation,

00:54:16.630 --> 00:54:19.780
how do you decide which
studies to include?

00:54:19.780 --> 00:54:22.863
And is there a panel of
experts, or some sort of expert

00:54:22.863 --> 00:54:23.362
[AUDIO OUT]?

00:54:23.362 --> 00:54:27.670
Can you address some
of that, perhaps?

00:54:27.670 --> 00:54:28.430
Pascale Gaudet: Sure.

00:54:28.430 --> 00:54:31.900
So in terms of
[AUDIO OUT], I think

00:54:31.900 --> 00:54:35.770
it really depends on the type
of data you're looking at.

00:54:35.770 --> 00:54:38.930
So for example, for
sequence information,

00:54:38.930 --> 00:54:43.000
you can certainly do that
by automatically making sure

00:54:43.000 --> 00:54:47.970
that the [AUDIO OUT]
you're annotating really

00:54:47.970 --> 00:54:52.430
corresponds to a position in the
referenced genome, for example.

00:54:52.430 --> 00:54:56.260
In terms of literature
curation, it's

00:54:56.260 --> 00:55:02.690
very hard to have net
that will catch problems

00:55:02.690 --> 00:55:06.370
that-- we certainly
have the same papers by,

00:55:06.370 --> 00:55:09.380
for example, Christophe
[? Decimau's ?] group where

00:55:09.380 --> 00:55:13.230
they compare the gene
ontology annotations made,

00:55:13.230 --> 00:55:14.990
predicted electronically.

00:55:14.990 --> 00:55:18.710
And then they compare
how often these

00:55:18.710 --> 00:55:22.320
were confirmed by experimental
annotations later.

00:55:22.320 --> 00:55:27.560
So that also helps to do some
what we call sanity checks.

00:55:27.560 --> 00:55:31.325
It's true that the quality
controls are very important.

00:55:31.325 --> 00:55:34.416
 

00:55:34.416 --> 00:55:35.790
Jack Van Horn: One of the other
