00:56:09.100 --> 00:56:11.870
Pascale Gaudet: Yeah, it's funny because I was reading articles about this

00:56:11.870 --> 00:56:15.740
recently, and my
experience-- and it's

00:56:15.740 --> 00:56:17.820
consistent with what
I was reading-- is

00:56:17.820 --> 00:56:20.410
it's not sufficient.

00:56:20.410 --> 00:56:23.790
It's true that
[AUDIO OUT] sciences,

00:56:23.790 --> 00:56:28.540
we started to develop tools and
infrastructure independently,

00:56:28.540 --> 00:56:31.510
really with the life
sciences in mind.

00:56:31.510 --> 00:56:35.520
But definitely,
we can [AUDIO OUT]

00:56:35.520 --> 00:56:40.380
[? college ?] that library
and scientists have

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:41.760
accumulated over the years.

00:56:41.760 --> 00:56:43.710
So This is also
another direction

00:56:43.710 --> 00:56:47.444
that this community should
take, in my opinion.

00:56:47.444 --> 00:56:49.610
Jack Van Horn: Yeah, I mean, it seems
like-- and somebody had asked

00:56:49.610 --> 00:56:52.900
a question about [AUDIO OUT]
fundamental difference

00:56:52.900 --> 00:56:57.770
between [AUDIO OUT] archivist
[AUDIO OUT] stockpiling

00:56:57.770 --> 00:57:01.530
examples of data, versus
curating that data-- which

00:57:01.530 --> 00:57:05.900
is sort of an ongoing process of
continuing to go back and make

00:57:05.900 --> 00:57:08.230
sure that it's accurate and
up to date, ans as you say,

00:57:08.230 --> 00:57:12.830
versioned or dated, so that
people know how to use it.

00:57:12.830 --> 00:57:15.100
Would you mind commenting
just a little bit

00:57:15.100 --> 00:57:18.620
about that distinction, and
why that's so important?

00:57:18.620 --> 00:57:22.774
Pascale Gaudet: About why-- yeah, the
accumulation versus the-- yeah.

00:57:22.774 --> 00:57:25.560
 

00:57:25.560 --> 00:57:33.170
Well, I think this is
where-- the idea is we need

00:57:33.170 --> 00:57:36.180
to know what we know right now.

00:57:36.180 --> 00:57:38.090
I think this is
extremely important.

00:57:38.090 --> 00:57:42.580
And this is also as
much-- as hopeful

00:57:42.580 --> 00:57:46.070
as I am in terms of
the automated methods--

00:57:46.070 --> 00:57:48.580
that the automated
text mining can

00:57:48.580 --> 00:57:51.490
go through all the
literature, and all the data

00:57:51.490 --> 00:57:53.580
sets, and find relations.

00:57:53.580 --> 00:57:55.860
But they will not
be able to find

00:57:55.860 --> 00:58:01.212
what has been proven [AUDIO OUT]
which we currently know

00:58:01.212 --> 00:58:02.600
[AUDIO OUT].

00:58:02.600 --> 00:58:06.810
It's really a [INAUDIBLE],
especially more [AUDIO OUT].

00:58:06.810 --> 00:58:11.680
 

00:58:11.680 --> 00:58:12.990
Knowing what we know.

00:58:12.990 --> 00:58:19.080
And it's a very--
a very high goal,

00:58:19.080 --> 00:58:22.070
but it's very important,
in my opinion,

00:58:22.070 --> 00:58:24.720
in order to be able
to progress further.

00:58:24.720 --> 00:58:29.070
There was a paper that
dates from a few years,

00:58:29.070 --> 00:58:34.590
by John [? Yuan, ?] that says,
"why most published research

00:58:34.590 --> 00:58:35.810
findings are false."

00:58:35.810 --> 00:58:38.180
And that's probably
an exaggeration,

00:58:38.180 --> 00:58:41.530
but there's certainly a lot
of hype in the literature,

00:58:41.530 --> 00:58:45.120
and a lot of things
that never get

00:58:45.120 --> 00:58:48.695
produced once they're
published once.

00:58:48.695 --> 00:58:52.150
So it's great, but
if you [AUDIO OUT]

00:58:52.150 --> 00:58:57.420
have a real current
state of knowledge base,

00:58:57.420 --> 00:59:01.190
I think it's very difficult for
us, as a research community,

00:59:01.190 --> 00:59:06.750
to make progress in making
new, relevant discoveries.

